    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 191736\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1035 AM PDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry conditions through the weekend with around average \n  high temperatures\n\n* Chances of precipitation return Monday and Tuesday\n\n* Slight warming expected after the rain chances followed by cooling \n  in the late work week.\n  \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday.\n\nSpotty showers and storms in Lincoln Co look to dissipate by early \nSaturday morning as a ridging pattern focuses over the region. This \nchange in pattern will lead to drying conditions and reduce winds \nthrough the weekend. Highs look to stay right around average (mid \n90s to low 100s) with low temps closer to 5 degrees above average \n(in the mid 70s to low 80s.)\n\nThe ridging pattern looks to quickly switch to a troughing pattern \ninto early Monday bringing breezier winds and chances for showers \nand possibly a few storms. Rain chances become more widespread \nthrough Monday afternoon and evening, but the overall feed of \nmoisture looks to be on the modest side as the trough axis only \nbriefly interacts with the coast before pulling into our area. This \nmeans that while our chances for rain look to be good, it looks to \nnot offer much in rainfall amounts. Rain chances look to last \nthrough Monday night and into Tuesday morning before pushing \nnortheast.\n\nThe upper-level pattern turns more neutral by the mid week, with some \nslight ridging that could help temperatures increase by only a few \ndegrees. A deeper tough forms along the west coast and begins to \napproach the region by the late week, offering a slight cool down. \nThis trough looks to break before it can center over Nevada, \npreventing any chances for rain crossing into the area at the end of \nthe forecast period.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill remain relatively light over the next 30 hours, with sustained \nspeeds remaining 10 knots or less and generally following typical \ndaily directional trends. There is a low chance (10 percent) of a \ngust to around 15 knots this afternoon during peak heating, but any \ngusts should be short lived. VFR conditions will prevail through the \nTAF period with mainly clear skies into tonight, then increasing \nhigh cloud cover on Sunday. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds across the region \nwill remain relatively light this afternoon into Sunday, with \nsustained speeds of 12 knots or less. Directions will favor typical \ndaily directional trends in most locations. VFR conditions will \nprevail through the TAF period with high clouds increasing from west \nto east overnight and Sunday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock \nAVIATION...Planz \n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage: \nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
